d in what t  cells can do in terms of what kind of  functions  they can they can do so what kind of  cytokines  they can express or whether they can  kill  we're also very interested in  trafficking receptors such as  chemokine receptors and integrins that  are indicative or can  drive where t-cells are located um  and and there are a number of these  different markers and then lastly  there's  a whole range of phenotypic markers  associated with  dysfunction or such as exhaustion  markers and senescence markers and  things like that  and so being able to look at cells in a  high dimensional way  um has allowed us to then look at how  all these different categories of  diversity including antigen specificity  relate to one another and so our vision  is to  or my vision is to understand at the  same time what t cells are specific for  by focusing on  um their their t cell receptor antigen  specificity is indicated in this  crystal structure um and then at the  same time understanding what their  phenotypes are  um and we're and i'll show a number of  these umap plots in this  in this talk which are i'm sure  everybody's familiar with  um for this webinar series um  dimensionality reduction  plots for high dimensional data and this  is one that we generated many years ago  from healthy uh pbmc cd8 positive t  cells we've been very interested in sort  of understanding the  range of phenotypes for um t cells and  in this case  um cd8 positive t cells and then i  should mention that our the company that  we spun off immunoscape is  um really focusing on these aspects for  their immune profiling  as well and so the way that we look at  antigen specificity  is to use peptide mhc multimers and in  particular is tetramers  and the concept is that t cell receptor  can only  combine to peptide mhc as t-cells  recognize antigen but this interaction  is very weak  and has fast kinetics and so monomeric  recombinant peptide mhc does not bind  stably to t  cells whereas if you com