 humans and  we're challenged by the fact that we  only have access to particular  tissues at certain times and especially  we have access to blood  um and of course within any of these  tissues that we get a hold of  there's a huge range of of cells of  immune cell types  and it's not always clear which are  involved in the disease and what they're  doing  especially in blood you could imagine  that there's all kinds of different  things going on  in our body and so how do we know what  immune cells are actually responding to  the disease of interest  um and so we're definitely trying to  understand what we can learn from blood  versus tissue  um how we can focus on disease relevant  cells  and so our strategy has been to focus on  t cell antigen specificity because by  knowing  uh what the t cells are specific for uh  we can then  um know better that we're looking at the  cells that are relevant for the disease  and these this work the goal of this  work is to  track these cells um in the context of  um immunotherapy trials and studies to  look for  better biomarkers or protectors of  outcomes or things like that  um and so i'm definitely interested in  t cell diversity because uh when we  identify these cells that are specific  for a disease of relevance we want to  understand  uh what their profiles mean and actually  because t cells  have such a huge range of antigen  specificity it means that they also have  a very unique experience as they develop  and then as they  encounter their antigen or not in the um  in the context of  of any disease or or under normal  circumstances  and so t cells can can vary in in many  different categories so they can vary in  terms of whether they've  had antigen experience so they can  display a memory  or a factor or an i.e phenotype they  also vary in their abundance  so if there's an antigen exposure then a  t cell would  would expand and show signs of  proliferation and also be much more  abundant  and then we'd be interested in what t  cells can do in terms of what kind of  functions  they can they can do so what kind of  cytokines  they can express or whether they can  kill  we're also very interested in  trafficking receptors such as  chemokine receptors and integrins that  are indicative or can  drive where t-cells are located um  and and there are a number of these  different markers and then lastly  there's  a whole range of phenotypic markers  associated with  dysfunction or such as exhaustion  markers and senescence markers and  things like that  and so being able to look at cells in a  high dimensional way  um has allowed us to then look at how  all these different categories of  diversity including antigen specificity  relate to one another and so our vision  is to  or my vision is to understand at the  same time what t cells are specific for  by focusing on  um their their t cell receptor antigen  specificity is indicated in this  crystal structure um and then at the  same time understanding what their  phenotypes are  um and we're and i'll show a number of  these umap plots in this  in this talk which are i'm sure  everybody's familiar with  um for this webinar series um  dimensionality reduction  plots for high dimensional data and this  is one that we generated many years ago  from healthy uh pbmc cd8 positive t  cells we've been very interested in sort  of understanding the  range of phenotypes for um t cells and  in this case  um cd8 positive t cells and then i  should mention that our the company that  we spun off immunoscape is  um really focusing on these aspects for  their immune profiling  as well and so the way that we look at  antigen specificity  is to use peptide mhc multimers and in  particular is tetramers  and the concept is that t cell receptor  can only  combine to peptide mhc as t-cells  recognize antigen but this interaction  is very weak  and has fast kinetics and so monomeric  recombinant peptide mhc does not bind  stably to t  cells whereas if you complex it into  these multimers  there's cooperative binding and you can  get very good signal  to noise for staining t cells  based on what they're specific for the  nice thing about this is that you don't  have to  manipulate the cells you can look at  simply what they're specific for  and then in parallel assess what they  look like and what they're doing  and so the way that we apply this  further is to then multiplex  these peptide mhc tetramers and so we  developed this years ago  where a strategy that involves using  multiple  tags on the same antigen specificity of  a multimer  and then t cells that bind those tags  specifically will then be stained with a  unique combination  of tags and this works with both  fluorescence and  heavy metal and it's particularly  convenient for mass cytometry because in  this case  if we dedicate 10 heavy metals to  peptide mhc tetramers  it means that we can stain up to 120  different  t-cell antigen specificities at the same  time  and then we have 30 or more channels  remaining  to profile the antigen specific cells  that we detect with any of those  codes and so what we do in the lab is  then get  single pbmc or tissue samples  and then we stain them with these  multiplex libraries of tetramers that we  create  um and then at the same time we stain  them with  antibodies to profile the cells we run  them on the cytoph  and then we'll at the same time get this  deep phenotypic profile of cells  as well as um hits for any of the  tetramers that we've included in the  multiplex  tetramer staining library and just to  show an example of what this can look  like this is a healthy donor um  where it's at the top here we have a  umat plot of just cd8 t  cells with some example markers to give  you some orientation  of what they of what the the different  populations represent  and with our panel we can discriminate a  number of different  cd8 subsets and then on the bottom here  is overlay of the red dots for each of  these antigen specificities and so  what's cool is that  for this healthy donor here we can see  that they have t  cells specific for a number of different  epitopes derived from  these various viruses so adenovirus  epstein-barr virus flu  and then herpes simplex virus and then  we can see  the phenotypes of those cells and based  on those phenotypes we can have some  understanding of what that person's  immune response is doing  um for these for these different viruses  and it also shows that  different epitopes can have different  involvement  in phi t cells and so this is really  what we're trying to take advantage of  when we then go look at  patient samples and try to understand  what's happening  within it with a t cell response and the  nice thing is we always can include  these control epitopes to get some sense  of what  t cells are doing that are unrelated to  the disease so specific for other things  like these  these common viral infections i should  mention also that we're  it's quite nicely synergistic to use  this approach with a multi-omics  single cell sequencing methods that  we're doing more and more of i won't  talk about that much today  but we can use mass cytometry as part of  a screen to identify cell populations  and things that then we want to zoom in  further with single cell sequencing  um so this is just a quick example of a  paper we published  last year studying dengue  acute dengue infection uh for multiple  cohorts in singapore and what was nice  to hear about our approach is that we're  able to  first look at broadly profiling what  happens to lymphocytes in general  over the course of of dengue infection  and we noted of course that in acute  time points there's a massive  activation of many different lymphocyte  subsets  including t cells and k cells mate cells  gamma delta cells b  cells and with this sort of broad  profiling  with mass cytometry we can investigate  the characteristics of all those  different activated cell populations  and that's quantified here but then at  the later time points a lot of this  activation is lost and so it's difficult  to know  what to look at in those samples that  would be related to  um having dengue infection and so for  that we're using the  um the multiplex tetramer approach that  i mentioned  so screening for a large number of  previously  identified dengue epitopes and then we  can identify we were able to identify  dengue specific t cells at all these  different time points and here on the  top right  is an example of how we're able to then  track the profiles of these cells over  time  um and there's a number of different  things we commented about this in the  paper  but interestingly there's really two  major outcomes in terms of the  phenotypes  of dengue specific cells one that have  kind of a memory or stem cell memory  phenotype  and then another that has this more  terminally differentiated effector  phenotype and remarkably that an  including marker like c57 which is  typically a senescence marker  but then we show that those cells are  also long lived and were maintained  even out to a year or more  post-infection so that's kind of a sense  of the types of things that we can do  and especially good for studying acute  viral infection  and so we're involved in various ways in  a number of different  covid19 related projects and so this is  just a real snippet  of data that we've collected very  recently  we're not super fast on this but we're  showing in this case  um some sars kobe 2 specific cells that  we detected  with an h of a b7 tetramer and you can  see the phenotypes here  as compared to some cmv specific cells  that we detected in that same  uh participant sample and this was a big  effort you know to get  going with all of this so the names here  are are also very important and  i'll um thank them at the end as well  so um now i'm just going to move on to  the the main topic that i wanted to talk  about today  uh and that is tracking cancer-specific  immune responses  and you know there's a lot to talk about  so much going on  in cancer immunology but if you think  about this cancer immunity cycle  whereby you know t cell responses would  be primed in lymph nodes  circulate through the blood into the  tumor  and then respond to the tumor what we  have access to  to study these t cell responses are the  blood  and the tumor tissue and so we want to  know what are the tumor-specific cells  doing in each of these  tissues and can we identify them so  first focusing on the tumor infiltrating  cells  um this is a paper that we published  recently we've done  other other studies in mice as well but  this one i'll highlight  which is what's done by yannick simoni  in the lab and in collaboration with  many  others especially clinicians in  singapore  and what we did was to get a hold of  tumor infiltrating t  cells and use our approach to screen for  new antigen specific t  cells so screening for t cells specific  for mutations  uh in those tumors for which we had  exome sequences  and then at the same time we also looked  for t cells  specific for these various unrelated  antigen specificities that i mentioned  such as ebv flu and cmv and what was  striking is that even though we could  identify cases of tumor-specific cells  this was harder because it's screening  so many different epitopes  but what was easier to to identify were  a number of examples  of t cells specific for these unrelated  antigens  derived from evd cmd or flu  and then from this we were able to then  ask okay what's different about these t  cells that are bystanders so they're  specific  for something unrelated to cancer and  therefore  we think much less likely to be actually  tumor specific  um and what we noticed then was in  addition to their being  quite heterogeneous in their profiles  and overlapping  often times with phenotypes that we  would expect for tumor infiltrating  cells for instance they have  pd-1 and tinted and other markers  associated  with exhaustion we noticed that they  were  almost completely lacking cd39  expression  and then based on that we've  hypothesized that cd39  could be a good marker to delineate  these bystander cells from  bonafide tumor-specific cells and then  looking back at all the samples that we  analyzed on the bottom right here um we  notice an amazing heterogeneity and the  frequencies of  tumor infiltrating cv8 that express cd39  this implies that some people have  very very few tumor-specific cells  as a fraction of their total cd8 while  others have a much higher fraction  and so we're continuing to work with  this hypothesis and  uh we'll see whether it holds up but in  the meantime  it's a it's an opportunity then to look  at uh  what do um how can we distinguish  uh these unrelated antigen specific  cells from uh tumor-specific ones and so  there's a number of different things  that we're doing to follow up on this  but the one that i was going to mention  today is looking at  what about cd4 t cells and this is just  to say that  within the panels that we've run we can  also identify  cd4 t cells we can just distinguish  on t reg cells by fox t3 expression  and then this plot just shows that  actually for the tumor types in this  case lung and colorectal for which we  have  a pretty large number of samples uh  there are  there's abundant cd4 infiltrating t  cells  and then amongst those there's actually  quite high frequencies of  non-t reg cd4 t cell so of course  there's a lot  of work done into studying roles of t  reg cells and suppressing the tumor  microenvironment  but what's there as well are abundant  non-t-reg  cd4-t cells and here's an example  of a umap plot for cd4-t cells  in one colorectal cancer patients tumor  and you can see that um there's a  section of this umap plot that  delineates t-regs  um by fox d3 and cd25 expression  and then when you look at cd 39 which  was our interest based on the work on  cd8 you can see that 